<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salian Franks</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were the Frankish groups of the north-west, first recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toxandria">Toxandria</a>, in the present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, in the 4th century. In the 5th century their kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> and Cambrai, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, king of the Salian Franks of Tournai, they united the Franks and conquered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Their law code, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a>, is named after them.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Salians are named in the sources only in connection with the campaign of Julian, then Caesar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> under his cousin the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>, around 358 CE. Ammianus Marcellinus calls them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> whom custom calls the Salii. Zosimus, writing around 500 CE, says they had been driven out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and settled on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> delta. Some modern scholars think they descended from Frankish groups that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> allowed to remain there around 293 to 294 and in Texandria in 342. The origin of the name is uncertain; proposals include a Germanic word related to German Geselle, meaning companion, and a word for island dwellers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Julian's campaign was partly prompted by the Roman need to bring grain from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> safely up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The praetorian prefect Florentius had promised the peoples of the delta two thousand pounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for passage, and Julian refused to pay it and marched against the Salians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamavi">Chamavi</a>. In the winter of 357 to 358 a Salian deputation came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a>. Ammianus writes that Julian sent his general Severus along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Maas</a> to attack them suddenly, after which the Salians surrendered. Julian let them keep the land they had settled and took them into Roman service. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a> lists units such as the Salii seniores in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><p>In the first half of the fifth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under a king named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodio">Chlodio</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> and Cambrai, probably in the period 445 to 450. Chlodio is called only Frankish in the sources. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childeric-i">Childeric I</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, who gained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a>, were said to be related to him. Clovis made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> his capital and was baptised in 496, beginning the alliance of the Franks with the Catholic Church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Until the 1950s it was assumed that the Salians expanded south under that name and formed one of two great divisions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, the other being the Ribuarian Franks. This rests on the names of two law codes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-ripuaria">Lex Ripuaria</a> of about 630. Scholars now doubt that the term Salic referred to a specific tribe by the fifth century. The Salic law applied between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the Silva Carbonaria and was the earliest known code of the Merovingian kingdom. The Salian language is presumed ancestral to the Low Franconian dialects, which include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> dialects and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salian Franks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Toxandria, the <b>Salian Franks</b> farmed land between the Meuse and the Scheldt under Roman authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franks were a confederation of Germanic peoples who appear in Roman sources from the third century on the lower Rhine, and by the fifth century groups of <b>Salian Franks</b> had settled in the north of Roman Gaul, often as allies of the empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
